Open capture with the output's buffer size so shared-clock cards run duplex

With the capture-side fixes in place, the UMC1820 field install moved to
the next failure: the input stream now opens, and then the OUTPUT stream
build fails with 'snd_pcm_hw_params: Invalid argument (22)' - for the
configured buffer size and for the device-default fallback alike - and the
daemon panicked at the .expect. Cards in this class sync their playback
stream to the capture endpoint, so once capture is running, playback must
open with compatible parameters. Our capture stream opened with the device
default buffer while the output requested a fixed one, and the rates were
the only parameter we aligned.

Changes:

- Capture streams now open with the same buffer size as the output stream
  (audio.buffer_size, as clamped for the device), retrying with the device
  default if the input device rejects it - so both directions of a
  shared-clock interface run compatible parameters. InputStreamConfig
  carries the value; the stream builders take the final StreamConfig, and
  the ring buffer is created per build attempt.
- The output stream build no longer panics. Both the fixed-buffer attempt
  and the fallback report a diagnosed failure: the device name, the
  requested channels/rate/buffer, the parameters every open capture stream
  is running, and a note that shared-clock USB interfaces need both
  directions on the same sample rate and compatible buffer sizes; then the
  daemon exits cleanly.
- audio.buffer_size documentation notes it applies to capture streams too;
  CHANGELOG updated.

Verification: clean release build with zero warnings; full suite green
(507 lib + 28 http-api + 1 broker-restart + 26 streaming). The parameter
conflict itself requires shared-clock capture hardware, which this
development environment does not have; field confirmation comes from the
UMC1820 install switching its output back from a dmix-based workaround
device to plughw.

